---
import ExternalLink from '../components/ExternalLink.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import Pager from '../components/Pager.astro';
import TextSection from '../components/TextSection.astro';
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    ask: 'Is this site against AI?',
    answer:
      'Its view is that AI should do the work it does well, and that the people deciding should count everything a role did before replacing it. Cuts that held up are recorded alongside those that were reversed.',
  },
  {
    ask: 'Is a case a judgement of the organisation?',
    answer:
      'A case records what sources reported, with quotes. The doorman reading is marked as the site\u2019s own analysis and names the evidence it rests on. Motives are never guessed.',
  },
  {
    ask: 'Why are there so few cases?',
    answer: 'The ledger is new. The desk adds cases every week, and only those that pass every check.',
  },
  {
    ask: 'Can I use the cases?',
    answer:
      'Yes. They are published under CC BY 4.0: reuse them with credit to The Doorman Fallacy. The whole ledger can be downloaded from the ledger page.',
  },
  {
    ask: 'Who pays for this?',
    answer: 'Nobody. The site has no advertising and no sponsors. It is run by Jan Hallvard Larsen, and its code is open.',
  },
];

const contents = [
  { id: 'counts', label: 'What counts as a case' },
  { id: 'written', label: 'How a case is written' },
  { id: 'checks', label: 'The checks every case passes' },
  { id: 'layers', label: 'Reported facts and the doorman reading' },
  { id: 'following', label: 'Following up' },
  { id: 'corrections', label: 'Corrections' },
  { id: 'keeper', label: 'Who keeps it' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Common questions' },
] as const;
---

<Site
  title="How the ledger is kept"
  current="ledger"
  description="AI agents find, write and check every case under fixed rules. What counts as a case, how it is written, the checks it passes, and how it is kept up to date."
>
  <div class="page">
    <PageHead
      title="How the ledger is kept"
      lead="AI agents find, write and check every case, under fixed rules. No person reviews a case before it goes live, so the rules do that work. This page lists them."
      crumbs={[{ label: 'The ledger', href: '/ledger' }]}
      contents={contents}
    />

    <TextSection id="counts-title" anchor="counts" number={1} title="What counts as a case">
      <p>A case needs all three:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>A named organisation.</li>
        <li>Roles reduced: layoffs, contracts ended, or posts left unfilled on purpose.</li>
        <li>
          The organisation, or credible reporting, linking the reduction to AI or automation, backed by one
          primary source or two independent reports.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Rumours, forecasts and cuts with no named organisation are left out. When reporting questions an
        organisation&rsquo;s link to AI, the case records both, and draws no conclusion about motives.
      </p>
    </TextSection>

    <TextSection id="written-title" anchor="written" number={2} title="How a case is written">
      <p>
        A news sweep runs every week. A small, fast model reads each headline and sets aside the ones that
        cannot be cases. A larger model researches the rest and judges how well each one shows the question
        at the centre of the site: what else was the person doing?
      </p>
      <p>
        For each case worth writing, the sources are fetched, archived on the Wayback Machine, and split
        into numbered sentences. The writing agent writes statements and cites sentences by number. It never
        types a quote. Code copies each cited sentence into the case, word for word.
      </p>
    </TextSection>

    <TextSection id="checks-title" anchor="checks" number={3} title="The checks every case passes">
      <ol>
        <li>Every quote is found word for word on the archived copy of its source. No model is involved in this check.</li>
        <li>
          Every statement is checked against its quote by a separate model. A statement that says more than
          its quote, or suggests a motive, goes back to the writer once, and is removed if it still fails.
        </li>
        <li>Loaded words are not allowed.</li>
        <li>
          Every case has one primary source, such as the organisation&rsquo;s own statement, or two
          independent reports.
        </li>
        <li>The case keeps a fixed shape: the role, the change, what happened next, and open questions.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A case that fails is not published.</p>
    </TextSection>

    <TextSection id="layers-title" anchor="layers" number={4} title="Reported facts and the doorman reading">
      <p>
        Each case keeps two layers apart. What sources report is marked as reported. Which hidden work the
        change may have missed is the site&rsquo;s own reading, marked as analysis, and each point names the
        reported evidence it rests on.
      </p>
    </TextSection>

    <TextSection id="following-title" anchor="following" number={5} title="Following up">
      <p>
        A case is a record that keeps changing. Each one is checked again on a schedule set by its status. What
        each status means, and when it is checked, is on the ledger page under{' '}
        <a href="/ledger#statuses">Statuses and follow-up</a>.
      </p>
    </TextSection>

    <TextSection id="corrections-title" anchor="corrections" number={6} title="Corrections">
      <p>
        If something in a case is wrong, use Report an error on its page, or <a href="/suggest">suggest a case</a>.
        The desk checks the report against the sources. Corrections are listed on the case page, and every
        change to a case is public in its history on{' '}
        <ExternalLink href="https://github.com/jahala/doorman-fallacy">GitHub</ExternalLink>.
      </p>
    </TextSection>

    <TextSection id="keeper-title" anchor="keeper" number={7} title="Who keeps it">
      <p>
        The ledger is curated by <a href="/about">Jan Hallvard Larsen</a>. The desk uses Claude, by
        Anthropic, for research and writing, and Jev, by TypeSafe, for the fast checks. The code is open
        source under the{' '}
        <ExternalLink href="https://github.com/jahala/doorman-fallacy/blob/master/LICENSE">MIT licence</ExternalLink>;
        the cases and the text of this site are published under{' '}
        <ExternalLink href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</ExternalLink>, so you can
        reuse them with credit.
      </p>
    </TextSection>

    <TextSection id="faq-title" anchor="faq" number={8} title="Common questions">
      <dl class="questions">
        {
          questions.map(({ ask, answer }) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="t-subhead">{ask}</dt>
              <dd>{answer}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </TextSection>

    <Pager previous={{ label: 'The ledger', href: '/ledger' }} next={{ label: 'Suggest a case', href: '/suggest' }} />
  </div>
</Site>

<style>
  .questions {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  .questions dd {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
